{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Elohim spoke to Moses and said to him, I am YHWH.</b> \"To him\" is entirely superfluous, for he had already mentioned the name of Moses. And it appears to me along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (see Rashbam, Ibn Ezra, Sforno on Exodus 2:10) — that this is why he was called \"מֹשֶׁה\" and not \"מָשׁוּי,\" for he was drawn from the water — because \"מֹשֶׁה\" is in the active participle, and the spirit of YHWH spoke through the mouth of Pharaoh's daughter to call him \"מֹשֶׁה\" in the sense of drawing and pulling, for he is the one who draws Israel out of exile, out of the waters of brazenness. And if Moses had examined his own name, it would truly have become known to him that through him Israel would be redeemed, and he would not have picked a quarrel, saying, \"Why have You sent me, why have You done harm,\" and so forth. However, from another side one may learn merit on Moses' behalf: he examined his own essence and saw himself as heavy of mouth and tongue; therefore he thought himself unworthy of this mission. For this reason it says \"וַיְדַבֵּר\" — a hard speech, \"אֱלֹהִים\" — this is the attribute of strict justice, \"אֶל מֹשֶׁה\" — to say that he is worthy to have judgment brought upon him, for he did not examine his name \"מֹשֶׁה\" and understand from within it that he would be the one who draws and pulls Israel out of exile. And from this side he should not have said, \"Why have You done harm.\" However, with regard to his essential nature, HaShem filled with compassion upon him: \"וַיֹּאמֶר\" — a soft utterance, \"אֵלָיו\" — on account of his essential nature, for since he was heavy of mouth and tongue, his heart was therefore emboldened to say, \"Why have You sent me.\" And for the sake of \"אֵלָיו\" there was said to him \"אֲנִי ה׳\" which denotes compassion, for his name and his essence are contradictory to one another. Therefore He said \"אֲנִי ה׳,\" full of compassion, to judge you for the merit."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The abbreviation פס״ז in the source has been resolved as רשב״ם (Rashbam) based on the names cited (Ibn Ezra, Sforno) and the reference to Exodus 2:10; it may alternatively refer to רשב״א."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:31.080302727Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}